          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How Quantities Combine: Understanding Data"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.853,
            0.071,
            0.881,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.11,
            0.883,
            0.167
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The concept of weighted arithmetic mean was also used by Indian mathematicians like Śrīdharācārya (c. 750 CE) to estimate the purity of gold-alloys (essentially today's karat calculations)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.166,
            0.883,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The concept of the weighted mean eventually appeared in other parts of the world, e.g., Europe, where it gained prominence by the 1700s. The weighted mean now plays a crucial role across mathematics and statistics, allowing us to combine data of different sizes or importance into one number to give an overall accurate picture."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "doc_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.366,
            0.287,
            0.629,
            0.315
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise Set 10.1"
            }
          ],
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.335,
            0.883,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. The average score of students on a test in Section A is 72 and that of students in Section B is 76. What is the combined average of both the sections given that Section A has 30 students and Section B has 25 students?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.413,
            0.882,
            0.487
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. A farmer mixes three equal quantities of fertilisers. The first one contains "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 } { 1 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " nitrogen, the second contains "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 9 } { 5 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " nitrogen, and the third contains "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 3 } { 6 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " nitrogen. What is the fraction of nitrogen in the mixture?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.487,
            0.882,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. (Śrīdharācārya, Pāṭīgaṇita, c. 750 CE) In ancient India, Varṇa was the measure of gold purity. A purity of 16 varṇa meant pure gold; in general, a purity of k varṇa meant that the gold-alloy was k/16 gold and the rest impurities. (Now the term used is karat; 16 Varna = 24 karat.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.581,
            0.881,
            0.639
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Suppose a goldsmith melts together three pieces of gold: 9 units at 12 varṇa, 5 units at 10 varṇa, and 17 units at 11 varṇa. Find the purity in varṇa of the combined gold."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.64,
            0.881,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The average rainfall per day in the months of May, June, and July in a certain location are 3.5 mm, 10 mm and 8.7 mm respectively. Write an expression that gives their combined average."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.698,
            0.869,
            0.718
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Calculate the concentration of spice mix in these two scenarios."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.197,
            0.717,
            0.882,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) A 100 mL kashayam/kadha with 5% spice mix, a 200 mL one with 10% spice mix, and a 300 mL one with 15% spice mix are combined."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.189,
            0.772,
            0.882,
            0.829
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) A 300 mL kashayam/kadha with 5% spice mix, a 200 mL one with 10% spice mix, and a 100 mL one with 15% spice mix are mixed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.846,
            0.402,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "10.1.3 Custom Weights"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.871,
            0.882,
            0.913
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The weighted mean is also used whenever parts of data require different levels of importance or weightage. Let us look at an example."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
